+      <article id="journal-article" class="journal-content">
+        <p>
+          Every bag of coffee we roast begins its journey thousands of miles from our roastery, nestled in high-altitude micro-climates across the coffee belt. Over the past decade, we have moved away from anonymous commodity exchanges in favor of direct-trade relationships with smallholder farmers and cooperatives in Ethiopia's Yirgacheffe region, Colombia's Huila highlands, and the volcanic slopes of Antigua, Guatemala. By visiting these partner farms during harvest season, we work side by side with growers to select specific varietals—such as Heirloom, Bourbon, and Gesha—grown under native shade canopies that protect local biodiversity and enrich the soil naturally.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Once the coffee cherries reach peak ripeness, they are hand-picked in multiple passes so only the sweetest fruit makes it to the mill. Depending on regional traditions and water availability, our partners use either crystal-clear mountain spring washed processing for crisp, floral clarity or honey and natural sun-drying on raised African beds to cultivate lush, jammy fruit complexity. Throughout fermentation and drying, moisture levels are monitored hourly until the parchment coffee stabilizes at an ideal 10.5% to 11.5% moisture content, ensuring the green beans lock in their delicate aromatic precursors before being sealed in GrainPro liners for ocean transit.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          When the green lots arrive at our roastery, our quality control team conducts rigorous physical grading and blind cupping sessions following Specialty Coffee Association protocols, accepting only lots that score 86 points or higher. Because we pay transparency-backed premiums well above Fair Trade minimums—often 40% to 60% higher than commodity market prices—farmers can reinvest in regenerative agriculture, wet-mill upgrades, and community wages. Finally, we craft custom small-batch roast profiles for each micro-lot, balancing heat transfer and Maillard development to honor the terroir and hard work behind every single bean.
+        </p>
+      </article>
